speechtext:
The hon. member is absolutely right; we should have more storage. But we can only provide it as we have the money voted to build elevators and to carry on that work, and we will do it as fast as we can. Anything that can be saved in the way of transportation or storage charges is saved to the farmer-no doubt about that. I go further, and I say I am heartily in accord with some arrangement -by nationalization of the railways if that [DOT]can be shown to be the most advantageous to get this railway system into such shape that we can operate the railways together and save a lot of money. Whatever you save, as I have said, is saved to the farmers of the West, and the people who have freight going over the railway system. Some people say that if the Government owned the railways so that there would be no looking for large dividends or profits, but operated them in the interests of the people, probably they could be operated at a great deal cheaper rate than at present. If that can be done	it will
certainly be a saving to the people who have products to transport. I look forward with a great deal of interest to the next session, when we shall be in a position to take up this great question -in fact, we must-and if a solution is arrived at, as I feel it will be, by taking over one or two of these systems, I believe that the cheaper transportation that probably will be got will have a great effect in the future so far as the development of the West is concerned. The question of increased accommodation at Quebec and at other ports will require consideration. The Montreal elevator has a capacity of 15,000,000 bushels, but that is a small quantity; Montreal will likely have to have increased accommodation. We should have large storage facilities at St. John and Halifax. At Portland and some of the other railway terminals the storage capacity is very large;

yet you could not get any grain through them this year; they were all filled to their utmost capacity. If the grain can be sent to Quebec and the elevators filled, there will be a saving of four or five cents a bushel, the cost of shipping from Quebec to Halifax or St. John; and that will go right into the farmer's pocket. If the farmer wishes to ship hig grain to Quebec and hold it until the spring, he will get that advantage.
